  "paper": "special-relativity",
  "argument": "arg-sr-transported-clock",
  "title": "Time on a clock carried along a path",
  "spoken": "An ideal clock's elapsed proper time is the path integral of the square root of one minus its speed squared over c squared.",
  "explanation": "Add up, moment by moment, the rate at which the carried clock runs: one when it is at rest, less when it moves. Two legs at 0.6c lasting 10 stationary-system seconds give the travelling clock 8 seconds; a clock left at rest records 10.",
  "review": "draft",
  "notation": "modern-pedagogical",
  "unitSystem": "si",
  "assumptions": [
    "An ideal clock carried along a path through the stationary system, with speed u(t) below c at every moment.",
    "Flat spacetime, no gravity; the turnaround of a round trip is treated as instantaneous.",
    "Modern teaching notation: Delta tau is the modern proper time, the time the carried clock records, not the paper's tau."
  ],
